librecomp: hand over a thread's registers while it runs

`run_thread_function` is where a thread's register file is made, and it is a
local: it exists exactly as long as that call does. The map in ultramodern that
something outside the game reads it through therefore has to be given it here,
and taken back here -- including when the call ends by being thrown out of,
which is how a thread that destroys itself leaves.

Handing a saved state a pointer to the stack of a thread that has gone is the
one way this can be wrong, so the removal is a destructor rather than a line at
the end.

--- a/librecomp/src/recomp.cpp
+++ b/librecomp/src/recomp.cpp
@@ -526,6 +526,18 @@
     if (game_entry.thread_create_callback != nullptr) {
         game_entry.thread_create_callback(rdram, &ctx);
     }
+
+    // Say where this thread keeps the processor, for as long as it has one.
+    //
+    // This context is the thread's register file and it lives on this stack, so
+    // it exists exactly as long as this call does -- including when the call
+    // ends by being thrown out of, which is how a thread that destroys itself
+    // leaves. Whoever is reading the registers of a parked thread must not be
+    // handed the stack of a thread that has gone.
+    struct RegistersWhileRunning {
+        ~RegistersWhileRunning() { ultramodern::forget_thread_registers(ultramodern::this_thread()); }
+    } registers_while_running;
+    ultramodern::set_thread_registers(ultramodern::this_thread(), &ctx);
 
     recomp_func_t* func = get_function(addr);
     func(rdram, &ctx);
